body:
> Comparison of the effect that https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/62547 claimed to mitigate:

This comparison shows the problem quite clearly. Look at how much the light bleeds around the sides of the sphere in the after photo. 

That being said https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/62547 is an approximation of an approximation. So we don't need to keep it around if it is doing more harm than good. From what I see here though, the problem this PR fixes appears more at lower roughness, whereas, the purpose of https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/62547 is to solve a problem that only appears at high roughness. 

Frostbite recommends the following:
```
float3 getSpecularDominantDir ( float3 N , float3 R , float roughness ) {
    float smoothness = saturate (1 - roughness );
    float lerpFactor = smoothness * ( sqrt ( smoothness ) + roughness );
    // The result is not normalized as we fetch in a cubemap
    return lerp (N , R , lerpFactor );
}
```

_Edit: Actually looking at our code and the filament code makes me think that this might be a linear roughness vs. perceptual roughness issue. It looks like Filament is using linear roughness while we are using perceptual roughness. The difference would be most visible at low roughness since `linear_roughness == perceptual_roughness * perceptual_roughness`_
